---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import PowerBIEmbed from '../../components/PowerBIEmbed.astro';
import RetailDashboard from '../../components/RetailDashboard.astro';

const measures = [
  ['Total Sales', 'SUM ( Orders[Sales] )'],
  ['Total Profit', 'SUM ( Orders[Profit] )'],
  ['Profit Margin %', 'DIVIDE ( SUM ( Orders[Profit] ), SUM ( Orders[Sales] ) )'],
  ['Orders', 'DISTINCTCOUNT ( Orders[Order ID] )'],
  ['Customers', 'DISTINCTCOUNT ( Orders[Customer ID] )'],
  ['Average Order Value', 'DIVIDE ( [Total Sales], [Orders] )'],
  ['Orders per Customer', 'DIVIDE ( [Orders], [Customers] )'],
  ['Returned Orders', 'CALCULATE ( [Orders], Orders[Is Returned] = "Yes" )'],
  ['Return Rate %', 'DIVIDE ( [Returned Orders], [Orders] )'],
  ['Repeat Customer %', 'DIVIDE ( [Repeat Customers], [Customers] )'],
  ['Average Days to Ship', 'AVERAGE ( Orders[Days to Ship] )'],
  ['Median Days to Ship', 'MEDIAN ( Orders[Days to Ship] )'],
];
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="retail-analytics"
  facts={[
    ['Data', 'Public Superstore sample: about 10,000 order lines, plus returns and regional managers'],
    ['Tools', 'Power BI Desktop, Power Query, DAX'],
    ['Report', 'Six pages, each with a desktop and a phone layout'],
  ]}
>  <PowerBIEmbed slug="retail-analytics" title="Retail analytics" />


  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Problem</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The brief asked for three dashboards from one retail workbook: one for management, one for regional
        representatives and one at state level. It listed what each had to show, down to top and bottom products by
        both sales and profit.
      </p>
      <p>
        My first build met most of it and was hard to read. Four product rankings and the whole shipping analysis
        shared one page, so each bar chart was 303 pixels wide and long product names were cut off.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">What I did</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I rebuilt it as six pages: a guide page, the management dashboard, two regional pages (sales and customers) and
        two state pages (products and operations). Splitting the regional and state dashboards in two took the product
        charts to 618 pixels wide, and the names read.
      </p>
      <p>
        I then checked the report against the brief line by line and closed three gaps. Bottom products by sales were
        missing. Profit trends were shown by region but not by state, so the management page gained a region and state
        matrix with a column per year. The returns tables had no return rate, so a product returned on four orders out
        of twenty looked the same as one returned on four out of four.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first build leaned on implicit totals, so cards read "Count of Customer ID" and nothing defined what an
        order was. The rebuild has 22 named measures in one table, each defined once and used on every page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <RetailDashboard />

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">Measures</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Twelve of the 22. The rest cover quantities, discounts, repeat customers, year-on-year change and captions.</p>
      <div class="scroll">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Measure</th>
              <th class="text">Definition</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              measures.map(([name, dax]) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{name}</td>
                  <td class="text">{dax}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split">
    <h2 class="label">What I would change</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The report answers what happened. It does not yet flag what needs attention. I would add a short list on the
        management page of sub-categories that sell well and lose money, since that is the first thing the profit chart
        shows.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
